#### Acess Specifiers in java
-- Access specifiers control the visibility and accessibility of classes, methods, variables, and constructors in Java.
-- They help achieve encapsulation, security, and controlled access.

**Types of Access Specifiers
-- Java has four access specifiers:
1. public
2. protected
3. default (no keyword)
4. private
-----------
1. Public
- Accessible from anywhere
- No access restriction
public class Example {
    public int x = 10;
    public void show() {
        System.out.println("Public access");
    }
}
2. Protected
- Accessible within:
- Same package
Subclasses (even in different packages)
class Parent {
    protected void display() {
        System.out.println("Protected access");
    }
}
=> Commonly used in inheritance
3. Default (Package-Private)
 -No keyword is used
- Accessible only within the same package
class Demo {
    void message() {   // default access
        System.out.println("Default access");
    }
}

=> Not accessible outside the package

4. Private
- Accessible only inside the same class
- Highest level of data hiding
class Test {
    private int data = 100;
    private void show() {
        System.out.println("Private access");
    }
}
